<!-- Managed by ansible (roles/setup/hypervisor). Edits here are overwritten. -->
<!--
  The staging guest's egress fence. One drop rule per production range — the LAN, the
  cluster's pod CIDR and its Service CIDR — applied at the guest's own tap device.

  DECIDED: a libvirt nwfilter, not a ufw `route deny` and not a FORWARD hook script.
  The first attempt (#508) was a ufw route rule on daniel-server. It deployed, `ufw status`
  listed it, and it was inert — the probe still reached the production VIP, the k3s API and
  daniel-pi's unauthenticated wg-easy UI from inside the guest. The proof it could never
  have worked: /etc/default/ufw already sets DEFAULT_FORWARD_POLICY="DROP", so if ufw's
  forward chain governed this traffic the guest would have been fenced before that rule was
  written. libvirt's own FORWARD accept is reached first.

  nwfilter removes the ordering question rather than arguing it: libvirt attaches these
  rules to the guest's tap device and manages their position itself, so there is no chain
  whose order a later reader has to verify. It is also the mechanism libvirt's own stock
  filters use for exactly this class of restriction — `no-ip-spoofing` filters a NAT
  network's guest traffic on this same path, which is the evidence this direction works
  here at all.

  DECIDED: drop one destination, do not allowlist. An allowlist filter would also have to
  name DHCP, DNS and the whole internet, and the first thing it would break is the internet
  egress staging legitimately needs — which the probe reports as a broken fence, correctly.
  A single drop rule with everything else falling through keeps the blast radius to the one
  thing being forbidden.

  DECIDED: blanket, with no exception for the in-cluster registry. An exception looks
  necessary because `ical-proxy` is in the staging subset to exercise the image-builder path
  end to end — but docs/staging-cluster.md Decision 8 already rules that traffic out:
  staging runs its OWN registry on daniel-stage, because a staging build pushing to the
  production registry could overwrite a tag production pulls.

  Guest -> daniel-server itself is unaffected: the guest reaches this host on the staging
  gateway address, which is not in the LAN CIDR. Ansible therefore still reaches the guest,
  and because the acceptance probe runs over exactly that path a fence that broke it would
  report UNREACHABLE rather than a clean pass.

  The acceptance gate, run on daniel-server (the only host that routes to the guest):

    uv run python scripts/diagnostics/staging_egress_probe.py

  It must report the internet still reachable and every production target refused. The shape
  of this filter and its attachment are ENFORCED by ansible/tests/staging/test_staging_egress_fence.py,
  but a filter that is defined and unattached reads identical to a working one from the host,
  so the probe is the thing that decides.
-->
<filter name='{{ hypervisor_staging_nwfilter_name }}' chain='root'>
  <!-- DECIDED: the UUID is pinned, derived from the filter's own name.
       `virsh nwfilter-define` does NOT replace an existing filter the way `net-define`
       replaces an existing network. Handed XML with no <uuid> it mints a fresh one, then
       refuses on the name collision: "filter 'x' already exists with uuid ...". Measured on
       daniel-server 2026-08-28 with a throwaway filter — the first define succeeds and every
       define after it fails, so a role that omitted this worked once and broke on re-run.
       `to_uuid` is a deterministic UUIDv5 over the name, so the rendered file is stable and
       the define is a genuine replace. A host carrying a filter of this name under some
       OTHER uuid (only reachable by defining one by hand) fails loudly here. Clearing it takes
       BOTH commands, in this order — libvirt refuses to undefine a filter a domain references,
       with "Requested operation is not valid: nwfilter is in use":

         virsh -c qemu:///system destroy {{ staging_vm_hostname }}
         virsh -c qemu:///system nwfilter-undefine {{ hypervisor_staging_nwfilter_name }}

       then re-run the role, which redefines the filter and starts the guest. That refusal is a
       feature: a referenced fence cannot be removed out from under a running guest. -->
  <uuid>{{ hypervisor_staging_nwfilter_name | to_uuid }}</uuid>
  <!-- DECIDED: the cluster's pod and Service ranges are fenced alongside the LAN, and the
       Service range is fenced even though it also contains the guest's OWN Services.
       lan_subnet alone left the whole cluster network open: measured from inside the guest on
       2026-08-28, prod's Longhorn API answered unauthenticated on its ClusterIP 10.43.152.244
       and on a longhorn-ui pod IP 10.42.1.216, exposing a mutating `diskUpdate` action, while
       the LAN rule correctly refused the MetalLB VIP. A ClusterIP is not on the LAN.

       Fencing the guest's own ranges is safe only while staging is a single node, and that is
       the assumption to check before adding a second one. Traffic to staging's own pods and
       Services is delivered on the guest's internal cni0 and by its own kube-proxy rules; it
       never crosses the tap device this filter attaches to, so these rules cannot see it.
       Add a staging agent node and pod-to-pod traffic starts crossing the wire, where a /16
       drop would break it. ansible/tests/staging/test_staging_egress_fence.py ties that assumption to
       k3s_agent_node_ips and fails when it stops holding. -->
{% for cidr in [lan_subnet, k3s_pod_cidr, k3s_service_cidr] %}
  <rule action='drop' direction='out' priority='100'>
    <ip dstipaddr='{{ cidr.split('/')[0] }}' dstipmask='{{ cidr.split('/')[1] }}'/>
  </rule>
{% endfor %}
</filter>
